{{!-- THREATS & DANGERS — moved here from the steading sheet. A threat is the GM's own prep,
      written for one pair of eyes, so it belongs on the GM's sheet rather than on the village
      the players share.

      The `steading-*` class names are kept DELIBERATELY. They are CSS hooks, not statements
      about where the panel lives: `.steading-prep-cards` is the shared card chrome (collapse
      chevron, the re-map from the standalone card's rem sizes onto this sheet's --st-fs-*
      tokens) that this tab and Sites both wear, and `.steading-threats` is the scoping ancestor
      roughly forty rules in stonetop.css hang off. Renaming them means rewriting that block,
      and every rule that stopped matching would fail silently as an oversized or unstyled card.
      The storage did not move either, which is the other half of the same story: these cards
      live in a journal pointed at by a flag on the STEADING actor. See gm-prep-tabs.js.

      NO SECTION PENCIL, and so no `stonetop-readonly`. This tab had both, and what the lock
      reached in the end was one button: the trash. Everything else on it writes nothing — a
      threat is written in its editor and a hazard in its wizard, the doom ticks are a play-time
      tracker, the collapse chevrons and the type reference below only fold and unfold prose — so
      each had to be exempted by name in stonetop.css, one at a time, and the type reference was
      never exempted at all and spent its whole life mouse-dead. Deleting is guarded by the
      confirm dialog in `_confirmDeletePrepPage`. Sites says the same thing at its own head. --}}
<div class="tab threats" data-group="primary" data-tab="threats">
	<section class="sheet-tab steading-threats steading-prep-cards">

		{{#if stonetop.hasSteading}}
		<p class="steading-threats-intro">{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.threats.intro"}}</p>

		{{!-- One section per proximity tracker (Homefront / Nearby / Distant), each with its own
		      "write up" button that pre-seeds that proximity. --}}
		{{#each stonetop.threatGroups}}
		<div class="steading-threats-section">
			{{!-- Folded under the proximity's stable id ("homefront" / "nearby" / "distant"), not
			      its label: a label is display text, so a copy edit would silently drop the fold
			      and a blank one would leave a caret that does nothing.

			      The hint is what the tracker MEANS (Book I p.288), inline in the heading rather
			      than a line under it: the heading is the one part of a section a fold leaves on
			      screen, so a GM scanning three collapsed trackers for where a new threat belongs
			      can still read the answer. --}}
			{{> "stonetop.section-heading" headingClass="steading-residents-heading" title=heading note=hint collapse=id}}
			{{#if threats.length}}
			<div class="steading-threats-grid">
				{{#each threats}}
				<div class="threat-card-wrap" data-page-uuid="{{uuid}}">
					{{> "stonetop.threat-card" this}}
					{{> "stonetop.gm-prep-card-tools" kind="threat" uuid=uuid}}
				</div>
				{{/each}}
			</div>
			{{/if}}
			{{> "stonetop.gm-prep-add-bar" kind="threat" proximity=id label=addLabel}}
		</div>
		{{/each}}

		{{!-- Hazards: the environmental half of Book I "Dangers", one flat section after the
		      proximity groups (hazards have no proximity; they belong to places and
		      expeditions). Same card affordances as threats: drag-to-pin, collapse; the pen
		      reopens the Make-a-Hazard walkthrough pre-filled. --}}
		<div class="steading-threats-section steading-hazards-section">
			{{> "stonetop.section-heading" headingClass="steading-residents-heading" title=(localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.threats.hazards") collapse="hazards"}}
			<p class="steading-threats-intro">{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.threats.hazardsIntro"}}</p>
			{{#if stonetop.hazards.length}}
			<div class="steading-threats-grid">
				{{#each stonetop.hazards}}
				<div class="threat-card-wrap" data-page-uuid="{{uuid}}">
					{{> "stonetop.hazard-card" this}}
					{{> "stonetop.gm-prep-card-tools" kind="hazard" uuid=uuid}}
				</div>
				{{/each}}
			</div>
			{{/if}}
			{{> "stonetop.gm-prep-add-bar" kind="hazard" label=(localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.threats.addHazard")}}
		</div>
		{{else}}
		{{> "stonetop.gm-prep-no-steading" noun="Threats and hazards"}}
		{{/if}}

		{{!-- PREP REFERENCE — the Threats chapter's guidance, condensed, from
		      module/threats/threat-guidance.js. Reference only: nothing here reads or writes a
		      card, so it sits OUTSIDE the has-a-steading branch above and is drawn either way.
		      A world with no prep filed yet is a GM about to write their first threat, which is
		      exactly who this is for.

		      It goes at the BOTTOM, under the cards. The tab's job is the prep you have written;
		      this is the glance-down for while you are writing more, and above the cards it would
		      push them off the first screen. Each section carries its own fold caret, so a GM who
		      knows the chapter folds them once and the sheet remembers per user.

		      Each section is its OWN `.steading-threats-section` box. That is load bearing: the
		      fold walk claims a heading's following siblings until the next heading
		      (utils/section-editing.js), so these laid out in one flat run would let the first
		      caret swallow every section below it. --}}
		<div class="steading-threats-reference">

			{{#each stonetop.threatGuidance}}
			<div class="steading-threats-section steading-threats-guide">
				{{> "stonetop.section-heading" headingClass="steading-residents-heading" title=title note=note collapse=collapseId}}
				{{#if caution}}<p class="steading-threats-intro">{{caution}}</p>{{/if}}
				{{#each groups}}
				<div class="steading-threats-guide-group">
					{{#if label}}<h4 class="steading-threats-guide-label">{{label}}</h4>{{/if}}
					{{!-- Numbered only where the order is the point (the write-up steps). The
					      `ordered` flag rides on the SECTION, so a group inherits it through the
					      `../` walk out of this each. Only the LIST TAG differs between the two
					      branches; the rows come from one partial so they cannot drift apart.

					      `rows=items`, in the hash beside the label, NOT as the partial's
					      context: a hash merged into an array context flattens it to a plain
					      object and the partial's each then walks the label as a row. See the
					      partial's header. --}}
					{{#if ../ordered}}
					<ol class="steading-threats-guide-list is-ordered">{{> "stonetop.gm-prep-guide-items" rows=items optionalLabel=@root.stonetop.threatOptionalLabel}}</ol>
					{{else}}
					<ul class="steading-threats-guide-list">{{> "stonetop.gm-prep-guide-items" rows=items optionalLabel=@root.stonetop.threatOptionalLabel}}</ul>
					{{/if}}
				</div>
				{{/each}}
			</div>
			{{/each}}

			{{!-- The eight types, each with its blurb and its own GM moves behind a disclosure.
			      The same catalog the Write-Up-a-Threat dialog offers, read straight from
			      threat-types.js rather than restated here. That dialog is where you PICK a type
			      while writing a threat up; this is where you read what one already on the table
			      would do next, which is the question the book prints these lists for. Each row
			      carries its type's accent as the tick down its left edge, the one place the card
			      chrome uses colour.

			      The name is a real <button> for the same reason the GM move entries' is: Enter
			      and Space then work with no keydown handler of ours, and `aria-expanded` says
			      which way it is. --}}
			<div class="steading-threats-section steading-threats-guide">
				{{> "stonetop.section-heading" headingClass="steading-residents-heading"
				    title=(localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.threats.guide.types")
				    note=(localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.threats.guide.typesNote")
				    collapse="threatGuideTypes"}}
				<ul class="steading-threats-guide-list steading-threat-type-list">
					{{#each stonetop.threatTypeReference}}
					<li style="--threat-accent: {{accent}}">
						<button type="button" class="steading-threat-type-toggle" aria-expanded="false"
						        data-tooltip="{{../stonetop.threatTypeMovesLabel}}">
							<strong>{{label}}</strong>
						</button>
						<span>{{blurb}}</span>
						<ul class="steading-threat-type-moves" hidden>
							{{#each moves}}
							<li>{{this}}</li>
							{{/each}}
						</ul>
					</li>
					{{/each}}
				</ul>
			</div>

		</div>

	</section>
</div>
